\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For generic perturbations of Kerr initial data whose maximal globally hyperbolic developments have continuously extendible Cauchy horizons, are those developments nevertheless inextendible by Lorentzian metrics of regularity $C^0\cap W_{\mathrm{loc}}^{1,2}$? More generally, for generic asymptotically flat vacuum initial data, is the maximal globally hyperbolic development inextendible as a weak solution of the Einstein vacuum equations?
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2015/33}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
For generic perturbations of Kerr initial data whose maximal globally hyperbolic developments have continuously extendible Cauchy horizons, are those developments nevertheless inextendible by Lorentzian metrics of regularity $C^0\cap W_{\mathrm{loc}}^{1,2}$? More generally, for generic asymptotically flat vacuum initial data, is the maximal globally hyperbolic development inextendible as a weak solution of the Einstein vacuum equations?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 30002919 (OWR-13854-002). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
